message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "The Narrow Regime of Thermal-Aware Anytime Inference: When Early-Exit Selection Helps on Edge CPUs, and When It Does Not"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22163329"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Jacob"
    given-names: "Manu Nicholas"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-6589-6572"
repository-code: "https://github.com/manunicholasjacob/thermal-aware-anytime-inference"
url: "https://manunicholasjacob.com/"
abstract: >-
  Data and code for an operating-regime study of thermal-aware anytime (early-exit) inference on a
  Raspberry Pi 5 (Arm Cortex-A76). Under saturating load, exit depth does not control temperature;
  the thermal lever exists only at fixed sub-saturation rates. Under a binding thermal cap the
  adaptive controller is the only policy that is both thermally safe and more accurate than the
  safe fallback, at the cost of a 5.4x p99/p50 latency ratio. INT8 has its own narrow regime:
  QOperator export is a pessimization at every exit, correct QDQ export pays only from the second
  exit onward, and quantization compresses the exit ladder's latency span from 20.6x to 6.9x,
  shrinking the range the thermal controller trades against.
